course: dictionary
lesson: 12990
title: Expertise: knowing a lot about one thing
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Expertise is special skill or knowledge in a subject. It comes from study, training or experience, not from luck. «The plumber used his expertise to fix the leaking pipe quickly.» The plumber knows a lot about pipes because he has learned and practised, so expertise must mean that special knowledge.
explain: Use the clues in the sentence. The plumber fixed the pipe quickly, so he must be good at his job. Try your meaning in the sentence: «The plumber used his special knowledge to fix the leaking pipe quickly.» It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Expertise is an uncountable noun, so we do not say «an expertise». We say «expertise in» a subject, such as «her expertise in maths». A person with expertise is called an expert, and a person who does not have it may «lack the expertise» to do a job.
fact: Expertise means special skill or knowledge in a subject.
fact: Expertise comes from study, training or experience.
fact: «Expertise» is an uncountable noun: you say «some expertise», not «an expertise».
fact: «Expertise in» is followed by the subject, as in «expertise in dinosaurs».
fact: Pupils can share their expertise, such as a child who knows a lot about dinosaurs.
exercise: «The doctor used her medical expertise to help the sick boy.» What does expertise mean here? | A long holiday | Feeling very tired | A lucky guess | *Special skill and knowledge from training
exercise: Mia knows more about dinosaurs than anyone in class. What is her expertise? | Her favourite lunch | *Her special knowledge of dinosaurs | Her new red bag
exercise: «The team lacked the expertise to finish the robot.» What does lacked the expertise suggest? | They finished it very fast | They were very rich | They had too much skill | *They did not have the skill needed
test: «Scientific expertise helps us trust what we learn from experts.» What does scientific expertise mean? | Loud music in a lab | *Trustworthy knowledge from experts | A made-up story
test: Which sentence uses expertise correctly? | The bus has a expertise. | Yesterday expertise was blue. | *Grandma has great expertise in baking bread.
exam: Ravi says, «Expertise is just another word for luck.» What is wrong with his idea? | Expertise means luck, so he is right | Expertise is only for doctors | *Expertise comes from study, training or experience, not luck | Expertise is a kind of game
